{"id": "DcBXuHdBWgV", "title": "Video by krishnachaytanyaa", "description": "Autoscaling is how a backend decides how many servers to run when traffic climbs.\nEvery server has a capacity. You set a target load per server, divide the incoming traffic by that target, and round up. That number is your server count.\nWatch it happen: 12.3 requests per second across 3 servers is 82% load each. Divide by the target, round up, and 5 servers brings it to 49% each. Same traffic, more machines, less load on every one of them.\nThe honest part nobody shows you: autoscaling has a ceiling. At 7 servers there is nothing left to add, and the requests past that get dropped on the floor.\nThis is horizontal scaling, the thing Kubernetes HPA, AWS Auto Scaling, Cloud Run and Fargate all do for you.\nWhat is your max replica count set to, and did you actually choose that number or inherit it?\nFollow @krishnachaytanyaa for backend systems and system design explained visually.\n#systemdesign #autoscaling #kubernetes #devops #softwarearchitecture", "timestamp": 1786712405, "channel": "krishnachaytanyaa", "uploader": "Krishna Chaitanya | Solutions Architect", "uploader_id": "27131031756", "like_count": 408, "comment_count": 2, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "duration": 9.006999969482422, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1577614127249779a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 49.612, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr20-2.fna.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQMqy8v3P9G6J8arrPoCx4lAyrs_2P1gEWDs0JnK2WUEv3ZaUvAevYUznotXFZrMYp4IV5hBOyEge3UE9Fjk1YEaF1dUKGK60iSSUM0.mp4?_nc_cat=111&_nc_oc=AdrFsM0c3Z3-5W7MnaEyCcJd1aUBDLPwu8WF8iVAn95JMl1PaB9mKUESCC7Nfi3fpjrYKZ4g-dU4WOUbj0dTnKR7&_nc_sid=9ca052&_nc_ht=instagram.fblr20-2.fna.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=MK8R7SwfSzgQ7kNvwEXZQGI&%3D&ccb=17-1&_nc_gid=cSesj2imINIPAHyiNzTcFg&_nc_ss=7a22e&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQGLhlUfSxtBrp2IQciodYPSbUfMSFSZhO7eVATEjlSzqw&oe=6A832